MainiacJoe wrote:
> Please comment on whether I correctly understand how Excalibur affects
> demons and how they behave when hostile, or point out anything else I
> may have missed:
>
> Setup: Wield Excalibur, have extrinsic speed, have a cockatrice corpse
> unwielded but in main inventory, have a wand of digging in main
> inventory.
> 1.  Rest on the downstairs to what you suspect may be a major demon's
> lair.
> 2.  Wait for the speed system to give you a guaranteed free move.
> 3.  Descend the stairs (first move).
> 4.  If it is indeed a demon's lair, you are wielding Excalibur so it
> is generated hostile.
> 5.  Switch to wielding the cockatrice corpse (free move).  You don't
> know yet whether a named demon is on the level.
> 6.  Being covetous, the hostile demon will teleport to you as its
> first move if it's there.
> 7.  Whack him with the rubber chicken before he gets his next move.
> If he doesn't show up, unwield the corpse and proceed to explore the
> level.
> 8.  Won't work for Juiblex becaues he's stoning resistant, that's what
> the wand of digging is for.

No, this doesn't work, because although wielding Excalibur will make
the demons be generated hostile, they will still wait for you.  (So
they're not going to immediately teleport to you anyway.)

The only effect that wielding Excalibur has is that demon princes that would
normally offer you a bribe will be generated hostile instead.  (It has no
effect at all on always-hostile demons like Orcus or Juiblex.)  If the
purpose behind this exercise is to make things easier, it's much simpler to
just carry a few zorkmids around and bribe them.

Of course, the only bribable demon princes with lairs are Asmodeus and
Baalzebub, who are pushovers for just about any character that has made it to
Gehennom, so unless you're playing an unusual challenge game there's not
much point in preparing very much.


